{-
   Const generics over a vector - Implementation-Const-Generics.md §5's first row, and the gap
   Implementation-Vector.md §9.3 and `VecWidth.yana`'s header both name: *"the portable set is
   declared over `Vec(a)`, which is the natural form of its lane type, so `lane` cannot read out of
   an explicitly sized vector at all"*.

   `Vec(I16, 4)` is the natural form of nothing on any target this compiles for, so before this every
   one of the portable operations was unreachable on one - the count was matched and never bound, and
   a signature could not quantify over it. What closes it is one const parameter per declaration:
   `fn (n: Int) lane(vector: Vec(a, n), index: Int) -> a` in Core, and the same shape written here.

   `splat`, `zero` and `iota` are const parameters too, with a default of zero - §1.8. What decides
   their count is the *result*, which §1.7 names as one of the two things that supplies a const
   argument, so `zero() :: Vec(I16, 4)` is four lanes; the default answers where nothing does, and
   zero is the natural form already written down. They used to keep the natural form and nothing
   else, on the argument that a result supplies nothing - see `sink` below, which builds an
   explicitly sized vector from an integer literal through `FromInt` instead.

   **A const-generic vector body specializes.** `Vec(a, n)` is a register type by resolve's own ABI
   decision, and a register class needs a lane count, so §4.3's erased form is a loop over
   register-fulls with a masked store - the plan's step 7, which is not built. `bodyLowerable`
   declines such a body, which is the same staging every other gap in that walk uses; the goldens
   here are therefore specializations and there is no `genEnv` anywhere in them.

   Like `VecWidth.yana` this runs under Node and not natively: the x64 opcode list is
   Implementation-Vector.md stage 5 and nothing here helps with it.
-}

import Native

let &sink = 0 :: Vec(I16, 4)

-- §1.2, and the whole of §5's first row: a lane read out of a vector whose count the *argument*
-- states. `n` is bound by `matchType`'s array-and-vector arm recursing into the count.
@noinline
fn (n: Int) secondLane(v: Vec(I16, n)) -> I16 = lane(v, 1)

-- §1.6: the count as an ordinary value. It needs no expression form - the parameter has a type - and
-- what it produces is `countof`, which folds to the count once the call has decided it.
@noinline
fn (n: Int) widthOf(v: Vec(I16, n)) -> Int = n

-- The count kept across an operation, which is what `Widen`, `Narrow` and the rearrangements all do:
-- `n` appears three times in one signature and means one number in all of them.
@noinline
fn (n: Int) doubled(v: Vec(I16, n)) -> Vec(I16, n) = max(reverse(v), v)

{-
   The operators, which needed Implementation-Const-Generics.md §10 and not const generics.

   `v + v` needs `Num(Vec(I16, n))` on the context, and a constraint list used to take bare
   identifiers - `parseSimpleType` reads a head's parameter list - so a class over an *applied* type
   could not be written at all. It is written here now, and what makes it work is that nothing behind
   the grammar had to change: `ClassConstraint::args` was always a list of arbitrary types, and
   `hasClassRequirement` compares them by pointer against the interned `Vec(I16, n)` the call site
   left undecided. That comparison is what makes the requirement *findable*: the lane count is a
   const parameter of this signature, so the type the constraint names is one only this signature
   can build.

   The constraint is written *after* the const parameter it mentions and `sameCount` below writes it
   before, because §10.3's first pass declares every `Const` entry ahead of the rest. Either order is
   the same context.
-}
@noinline
fn (n: Int, Num(Vec(I16, n))) twice(v: Vec(I16, n)) -> Vec(I16, n) = v + v

@noinline
fn (Num(Vec(I16, n)), n: Int) sameCount(v: Vec(I16, n), w: Vec(I16, n)) -> Vec(I16, n) = v + w

-- A count that is *written* rather than a variable still binds against a const-generic signature,
-- which is what keeps the two spellings one feature rather than two.
@noinline
fn quadWidth(v: Vec(I16, 4)) -> Int = widthOf(v)

fn main() -> Int:
    let v = withLane(sink, 1, 9 :: I16)
    let wide = doubled(v)

    -- `sink` is four zero lanes, so `v` is `[0, 9, 0, 0]`, its reverse is `[0, 0, 9, 0]`, and the
    -- lanewise max of the two is `[0, 9, 9, 0]`. So 4 + 4 + 9 = 17. The write to the mutable global
    -- is what keeps the rest alive - with nothing observable the whole file folds to a constant.
    --
    -- `twice(v)` is `[0, 18, 0, 0]` and `sameCount(v, wide)` is `[0, 18, 9, 0]`, so lane 1 of the
    -- sum of the two is 36 and lane 2 is 9: 17 + 36 + 9 = 62.
    let summed = sameCount(twice(v), sameCount(v, wide))

    sink = wide
    return widthOf(v) + quadWidth(v) + ((secondLane(wide) :: I32) :: Int)
             + ((secondLane(summed) :: I32) :: Int)
             + ((lane(summed, 2) :: I32) :: Int)
